(67,'What is a 2D array? Explain the memory representation of 2D arrays in C.',5.00,'2188',0,'<p>👉An array is a collection of elements, all of the same type, stored in contiguous memory locations. It allows storing multiple values under a single variable name, and each element can be accessed using an index.</p><h3><br></h3><h3><strong>2D Array</strong></h3><p>👉A 2D array is an array of arrays, meaning it is a collection of elements arranged in rows and columns, like a matrix. It can be visualized as a table with rows and columns.</p><p>👉For example, a 2D array with 3 rows and 4 columns can be declared as:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">int arr[3][4];\r\n</pre><h3><br></h3><h3><strong>Memory Representation of 2D Array</strong></h3><p>👉The elements of a 2D array are stored in contiguous memory locations. There are two common ways to store a 2D array in memory:</p><ol><li>Row-major order:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elements are stored row by row.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">First all elements of row 0, then row 1, and so on.</li></ul><ol><li>Column-major order:</li></ol><ul><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">Elements are stored column by column.</li><li class=\"ql-indent-1\">First all elements of column 0, then column 1, and so on.</li></ul><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">👉</span>Most programming languages like C use row-major order.</p>'),
(68,'How to calculate the address of a 2-D array element? Explain row major order with example in C.',5.00,'2190',0,'<p><strong><u>Memory Representation of 2D Arrays in C</u></strong></p><p>👉In C, a two-dimensional array is conceptually represented as a matrix with rows and columns. However, in memory it is stored as a continuous linear sequence of elements.</p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">👉</span>This storage method is called <strong>Row Major Order</strong>. In row-major order, the elements of the array are stored <strong>row by row</strong>. </p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">👉</span>The elements of the <strong>first row are stored first</strong>, followed by the elements of the <strong>second row</strong>, and so on until all rows are stored.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Row Major Order with Example</strong></p><p>Example:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">int stud[4][2];\r\nThis means:\r\nRows = 4\r\nColumns = 2\r\n</pre><p><br></p><p>In memory it is stored like this:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">a[0][0] → a[0][1] → a[1][0] → .. → a[2][1] → a[3][0] → a[3][1]\r\n</pre><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Address Calculation Formula</u></strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">👉</span>To calculate the address of element <strong>a[i][j]</strong>, the compiler must skip all elements of the previous rows and then move to the required column of the current row.</p><p><br></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">👉</span>The general formula is:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">Address = Base Address + ((i × Total Columns) + j) × Size of Data Type\r\n</pre><p>Where:</p><ul><li><strong>Base Address</strong> = address of first element (a[0][0])</li><li><strong>i</strong> = row index</li><li><strong>j</strong> = column index</li><li><br></li><li><strong>Total Columns</strong> = number of columns in the array</li><li><strong>Size of Data Type</strong> = memory size of each element (e.g., 2 bytes or 4 bytes)</li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Example</strong></p><p>Consider the array:</p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">int a[3][4]\r\n</pre><p>Suppose:</p><ul><li>Base Address = <strong>65502</strong></li><li>Size of int = <strong>2 bytes</strong></li></ul><p><br></p><p>Find address of <strong>a[2][3]</strong></p><p><strong><u>Step 1: Set initial value</u></strong></p><p>Base Address = 65502</p><p>Size of int = 2 bytes </p><p>Total Columns = 4</p><p>i = 2</p><p>j = 3</p><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Step 2: Apply Formula</u></strong></p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">Address = 65502 + ((2 × 4) + 3) × 2\r\n</pre><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Step 3: Solve</u></strong></p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">(2 × 4) = 8\r\n8 + 3 = 11\r\n11 × 2 = 22\r\n\r\n</pre><p><br></p><p><strong><u>Step 4: Final Address</u></strong></p><pre class=\"ql-syntax\" spellcheck=\"false\">Address = 65502 + 22\r\nAddress = 65524\r\n</pre><p><strong>✅ </strong>Address of <code>a[2][3]</code> = <strong>65524</strong></p>'),
(69,'What is a 2D character array? Write different ways to initialize 2D character arrays in C.',5.00,'2189',0,''),
